import OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupport

/-!
# Equal exponents: support certificates for more index primes (data)

For each prime `l` in 211, 227, 229, 233, 239, 241, 251, 257, 269, 277, 281, 283, 293, 307, 311,
313, 317, 331 a certificate accepted by `EqSupport.supportCheck` with source bound `10 ^ 10` and
target count `7` (see `OddPerfect.EqualExponentSupport`). The certificates come from a search
outside Lean; for 227 and 307 the search used factors of the row of `l` from a public factor
table, which need no trust because the checker tests divisibility. Only the kernel check below is
trusted. Data module: it proves nothing about odd perfect numbers alone.

Restricted result; no published source was checked; novelty not verified.
-/

namespace OddPerfect.EqSupport

open OddPerfect.EqCount (big witnessOK)

